1
00:00:01,877 --> 00:00:03,378
Yeah, that's no problem. Pam.

2
00:00:03,504 --> 00:00:06,005
Sure. Pam, knock knock.

3
00:00:06,090 --> 00:00:07,882
I'm on the phone. I know you are. Knock knock.

4
00:00:07,966 --> 00:00:09,926
You can fax it over.

5
00:00:10,010 --> 00:00:14,097
Yeah, 5-7-0-5-5-5... 4-9-1...

6
00:00:14,181 --> 00:00:15,848
0-1... ...7-4...

7
00:00:15,933 --> 00:00:17,600
7-5. Five-ling.

8
00:00:17,768 --> 00:00:20,853
0-1 -7-5. Thank you. Bye-bye.

9
00:00:24,108 --> 00:00:25,441
They would never know it was me doing it.

10
00:00:25,526 --> 00:00:26,693
Here we go. Knock knock.

11
00:00:28,278 --> 00:00:29,529
Who's there?

12
00:00:29,613 --> 00:00:30,613
Buddha.

13
00:00:30,739 --> 00:00:33,032
Buddha who? Buddha this bread for me, won't you?

14
00:00:36,245 --> 00:00:38,996
Great. I need something to wipe my hand.

15
00:00:39,123 --> 00:00:41,332
Now, there's butter on my desk. That was melting.

16
00:00:41,417 --> 00:00:43,376
It was a classic. I got a knock knock joke.

17
00:00:43,460 --> 00:00:44,419
No. God.

18
00:00:44,503 --> 00:00:46,129
Michael, please, please, please.

19
00:00:46,797 --> 00:00:47,880
Please let me. All right.

20
00:00:47,965 --> 00:00:50,299
Knock knock. Who's there?

21
00:00:50,384 --> 00:00:51,759
KGB. KG...

22
00:00:51,844 --> 00:00:54,554
We will ask the questions. What the hell was that?

23
00:00:54,638 --> 00:00:56,180
- What are you doing? - What are you doing?

24
00:00:56,265 --> 00:00:59,100
Hey, hey! Stop it! Stop it! Hey! Come on! You like that? You like that?

25
00:00:59,184 --> 00:01:00,893
What are you doing? Mine was part of a hilarious joke!

26
00:01:00,978 --> 00:01:02,687
Mine was retribution. What are you doing?

27
00:01:02,771 --> 00:01:04,272
No more knock knock jokes.

28
00:01:04,398 --> 00:01:06,524
That's it. Ding dong.

29
00:01:06,608 --> 00:01:08,276
Who's there? KGB.

30
00:01:09,194 --> 00:01:10,445
Dwight, get the door. I'm not answering it.

31
00:01:10,571 --> 00:01:11,571
Answer the door. Ding dong.

32
00:01:11,655 --> 00:01:13,322
- No way. It's the KGB. - Ding dong.

33
00:01:13,407 --> 00:01:14,532
I'm not answering that. Yes, you're going to.

34
00:01:14,616 --> 00:01:15,783
You answer it! I'm not going to answer it.

35
00:01:15,868 --> 00:01:17,452
Ding dong. I'm not going to answer it. It's the KGB.

36
00:01:17,536 --> 00:01:19,370
The KGB will wait for no one!

37
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:24,333
It's true.

38
00:01:49,985 --> 00:01:51,194
Good morning, Michael.

39
00:01:51,278 --> 00:01:53,196
No, no, no.

40
00:01:53,280 --> 00:01:57,909
I am not Michael. I am Willy Wonka!

41
00:01:59,328 --> 00:02:01,204
Good morning, Mr. Wonka. Here are your messages.

42
00:02:01,288 --> 00:02:02,580
Why, thank you very much.

43
00:02:02,664 --> 00:02:04,874
What are those? What are those? Tell me, please.

44
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:06,626
Jellybeans. No, no, no.

45
00:02:06,710 --> 00:02:09,420
They are not just ordinary jellybeans, little girl.

46
00:02:09,505 --> 00:02:14,383
These are extraordinary jellybeans.

47
00:02:14,676 --> 00:02:17,720
The Willy Wonka golden-ticket promotional idea

48
00:02:17,805 --> 00:02:20,890
is probably the best idea I have ever had.

49
00:02:20,974 --> 00:02:23,017
It's probably the best idea anybody's ever had.

50
00:02:23,393 --> 00:02:24,519
Three days ago,

51
00:02:24,603 --> 00:02:28,689
I slipped five pieces of gold paper into random paper shipments.

52
00:02:28,774 --> 00:02:32,568
This entitles the customer to 10% off of their total order.

53
00:02:32,653 --> 00:02:38,199
It will be a day for them that is full of whimsy and full of excitement.

54
00:02:38,951 --> 00:02:40,743
And full of fantasy.

55
00:02:42,079 --> 00:02:43,287
I've written these things

56
00:02:43,372 --> 00:02:46,999
because it is my responsibility as manager of this branch

57
00:02:47,084 --> 00:02:49,710
to profiligate great ideas.

58
00:02:49,795 --> 00:02:53,506
And I think I have done my part with the golden-ticket promotion.

59
00:02:53,590 --> 00:02:55,049
Now it is your turn.

60
00:02:55,133 --> 00:02:59,470
I want to hear some great ideas from you that are just as good as mine.

61
00:03:01,265 --> 00:03:02,974
We own our own delivery trucks.

62
00:03:03,100 --> 00:03:05,059
We could lease them out on the weekends... Too many words.

63
00:03:05,143 --> 00:03:08,145
Good ideas are simple, golden ticket.

64
00:03:08,230 --> 00:03:10,439
Free paper. No, Jim.

65
00:03:10,524 --> 00:03:14,235
We're a business. Post-its, that is a golden-ticket idea.

66
00:03:14,319 --> 00:03:18,573
NASA took five or six golden-ticket ideas to get men on the moon.

67
00:03:18,657 --> 00:03:23,035
Golden Girls. That's a golden-ticket idea. Right?

68
00:03:23,120 --> 00:03:24,620
I mean, how great was that show?

69
00:03:24,705 --> 00:03:27,707
Golden Grahams. Another. It's a...

70
00:03:29,459 --> 00:03:30,459
I don't get this.

71
00:03:30,544 --> 00:03:32,128
No, you don't. No. It...

72
00:03:33,714 --> 00:03:37,550
What will be the state of this company

73
00:03:37,634 --> 00:03:41,554
if I am the only one coming up with the great ideas, right?

74
00:03:41,805 --> 00:03:43,848
I think I should call her.

75
00:03:44,850 --> 00:03:47,685
No. No. No.

76
00:03:48,520 --> 00:03:52,231
Why is it so bad for me to call and ask her to lunch today?

77
00:03:52,566 --> 00:03:55,776
You're making it too easy for her.

78
00:03:55,861 --> 00:03:59,697
You're just conveying, "I like you just the way you are."

79
00:03:59,781 --> 00:04:01,741
But I do like her just the way she is.

80
00:04:01,825 --> 00:04:03,826
Well, that's not what we agreed on.

81
00:04:03,994 --> 00:04:05,077
What are you doing?

82
00:04:05,162 --> 00:04:06,954
Why don't you just go on a date with her?

83
00:04:07,039 --> 00:04:09,957
Try to spark up an ongoing joke and then, in a month or two,

84
00:04:10,083 --> 00:04:11,709
if it feels right, you'll know. No.

85
00:04:11,793 --> 00:04:13,419
You don't have to wait that long.

86
00:04:13,503 --> 00:04:17,214
I mean, you don't have to wait a month to ask her out. Just ask her out.

87
00:04:17,299 --> 00:04:19,592
You're asking him to give up all of his power.

88
00:04:19,676 --> 00:04:20,718
What power?

89
00:04:20,802 --> 00:04:25,222
Andy, Pam and Jim are all telling me

90
00:04:25,307 --> 00:04:28,059
how to deal with this girl that I like, Lynn.

91
00:04:28,518 --> 00:04:32,521
I don't like getting advice from more than one person at a time.

92
00:04:32,606 --> 00:04:34,815
I'm a textbook over-thinker.

93
00:04:35,359 --> 00:04:39,695
You can't let a girl feel good about herself. It will backfire on you.

94
00:04:39,780 --> 00:04:41,572
Every compliment has to be backhanded.

95
00:04:41,657 --> 00:04:44,867
"I like your dress, but I'd like it more if you had prettier hair."

96
00:04:44,952 --> 00:04:46,786
That's psychotic.

97
00:04:46,870 --> 00:04:48,120
Do guys actually do that?

98
00:04:48,205 --> 00:04:50,414
Guys with girlfriends don't.

99
00:04:50,499 --> 00:04:51,666
That's low, Tuna.

100
00:04:52,250 --> 00:04:54,293
Hey, Tom. What's that?

101
00:04:54,378 --> 00:04:55,836
Oh, you found a golden ticket.

102
00:04:55,921 --> 00:04:57,463
One of my clients found a golden ticket.

103
00:04:58,674 --> 00:04:59,674
Yeah.

104
00:05:00,008 --> 00:05:03,052
No, congratulations about that.

105
00:05:05,097 --> 00:05:06,430
Yeah, I'll take that down right now. Tell me,

106
00:05:06,515 --> 00:05:08,933
was it a spoiled little girl

107
00:05:09,017 --> 00:05:10,226
with big lips? I'm on the phone.

108
00:05:10,310 --> 00:05:13,270
Or an odd little boy with a cowboy obsession?

109
00:05:13,355 --> 00:05:14,772
Hold on one sec. Invite them on the tour.

110
00:05:14,856 --> 00:05:15,982
Hold on one sec.

111
00:05:16,066 --> 00:05:19,068
It's actually Blue Cross from Pennsylvania. Really?

112
00:05:19,194 --> 00:05:21,153
Yeah. That's kind of a big client.

113
00:05:21,238 --> 00:05:22,989
Hey, Oscar.

114
00:05:26,243 --> 00:05:29,662
How much of a hit is 10% of our Blue Cross account?

115
00:05:29,746 --> 00:05:30,746
10%? Yeah.

116
00:05:30,831 --> 00:05:32,164
They're our largest client. Yeah.

117
00:05:32,249 --> 00:05:34,125
- That's going to hurt. - Hold on one sec.

118
00:05:34,209 --> 00:05:36,002
Hold on one sec, Tom. What's that?

119
00:05:36,086 --> 00:05:38,379
You found five golden tickets?

120
00:05:39,923 --> 00:05:41,882
And does it say limit one per customer?

121
00:05:43,093 --> 00:05:44,719
Nope. It doesn't.

122
00:05:46,430 --> 00:05:48,848
How do you not spread out the tickets into different shipments?

123
00:05:48,932 --> 00:05:50,558
I thought I did,

124
00:05:50,600 --> 00:05:52,893
so no harm, no foul. Okay, well I'm going to go call corporate

125
00:05:52,978 --> 00:05:55,354
to make sure that they know I didn't lose half my sales.

126
00:05:55,439 --> 00:05:59,191
You didn't. It'll be fine.

127
00:05:59,276 --> 00:06:00,776
It'll be good.

128
00:06:02,070 --> 00:06:07,074
Hey, hey, hey. You idiot.

129
00:06:07,159 --> 00:06:08,367
Start over.

130
00:06:08,452 --> 00:06:13,539
Sir, I placed a bunch of golden tickets into five separate boxes

131
00:06:13,957 --> 00:06:16,500
and somehow they all ended up at Blue Cross.

132
00:06:16,585 --> 00:06:17,752
How does this happen?

133
00:06:17,836 --> 00:06:19,128
Were the boxes near each other?

134
00:06:19,212 --> 00:06:20,212
Irrelevant.

135
00:06:20,297 --> 00:06:23,466
I put three pallets on the truck to Blue Cross once a week.

136
00:06:23,550 --> 00:06:25,384
They use a lot of paper.

137
00:06:25,469 --> 00:06:28,888
Okay, I'm going to ask you something, and I want you to be honest.

138
00:06:28,972 --> 00:06:30,639
What is a pallet?

139
00:06:31,767 --> 00:06:35,061
My golden-ticket idea, okay.

140
00:06:35,145 --> 00:06:38,731
Why would anyone think that this is my golden-ticket idea?

141
00:06:38,815 --> 00:06:43,778
There's a one in 13 chance that this could be anybody's golden-ticket idea.

142
00:06:43,862 --> 00:06:45,071
That...

143
00:06:46,490 --> 00:06:48,616
Dunder Mifflin, this is Pam.

144
00:06:49,201 --> 00:06:50,826
Hi, David.

145
00:06:51,369 --> 00:06:55,581
No, I'm sorry. He's not back from the civil rights rally.

146
00:06:56,833 --> 00:07:00,127
I'll have him call you the minute he gets back from the Lincoln Memorial.

147
00:07:00,212 --> 00:07:04,757
When Michael's skirting a phone call, he gave me a list of places to say he is.

148
00:07:04,841 --> 00:07:06,592
"Stopping a fight in the parking lot.

149
00:07:06,676 --> 00:07:10,179
"An Obama fashion show." Whatever that is.

150
00:07:10,972 --> 00:07:14,767
Or "Trapped in an oil painting." I'm going to save that one.

151
00:07:17,979 --> 00:07:21,398
Nobody panic. The good news is

152
00:07:21,525 --> 00:07:23,692
they can't fire all of us, right? They can.

153
00:07:23,777 --> 00:07:25,194
No, they can't. Yes, they can.

154
00:07:25,278 --> 00:07:28,030
Well, Oscar, you don't know what the hell you're talking about.

155
00:07:28,115 --> 00:07:30,282
Michael, what do you think shutting down a branch is?

156
00:07:30,367 --> 00:07:32,618
All right, then we're screwed.

157
00:07:33,537 --> 00:07:39,708
I am just a net that traps all of your crappy subconscious ideas

158
00:07:40,794 --> 00:07:44,964
and adds a little bit of my own childhood memories and whimsy so...

159
00:07:45,048 --> 00:07:47,925
Okay, well, I lost a ton of money today.

160
00:07:48,009 --> 00:07:49,510
And I have a mortgage, so I'm a little pissed, too.

161
00:07:49,594 --> 00:07:50,928
Thank you. Jim is with me.

162
00:07:51,012 --> 00:07:52,680
Absolutely not. I'm mad at you.

163
00:07:52,889 --> 00:07:56,892
Well, you know what, Jim, it is not my fault that you bought a house to impress Pam.

164
00:07:56,977 --> 00:07:59,520
- That is why carnations exist. - That's not why.

165
00:07:59,604 --> 00:08:04,316
Okay, we need a gold-ticket idea to get us out of this mess. Yes?

166
00:08:04,401 --> 00:08:06,652
Does that mean an idea that blows up in our faces later?

167
00:08:06,778 --> 00:08:07,778
Good one.

168
00:08:07,863 --> 00:08:10,531
Okay, don't get that. Please.

169
00:08:10,907 --> 00:08:12,575
I've got a golden-ticket idea.

170
00:08:12,659 --> 00:08:15,202
Why don't you skip on up to the roof and jump off?

171
00:08:15,287 --> 00:08:16,954
That is not constructive.

172
00:08:17,372 --> 00:08:18,998
Dunder Mifflin, this is Pam.

173
00:08:19,916 --> 00:08:21,417
Oh, hi, David.

174
00:08:22,460 --> 00:08:24,503
He's having a colonoscopy.

175
00:08:26,089 --> 00:08:28,924
All right. I'll find out if he's out yet.

176
00:08:29,801 --> 00:08:32,052
Hello, David. I just got back from my procedure, so...

177
00:08:32,137 --> 00:08:34,263
Michael, what the hell is going on here?

178
00:08:34,347 --> 00:08:35,431
How are you doing?

179
00:08:35,515 --> 00:08:37,474
Fine. What is going on? My colonoscopy

180
00:08:37,559 --> 00:08:40,811
was an examination of my large colon

181
00:08:40,896 --> 00:08:43,689
and the distal part of my small bowel with a camera.

182
00:08:43,773 --> 00:08:47,234
Why did you okay this golden-ticket idea?

183
00:08:47,319 --> 00:08:50,196
We're going to lose a fortune, Michael. How could you let this happen?

184
00:08:50,280 --> 00:08:55,784
Well, there is an explanation that involves me not letting it happen,

185
00:08:55,869 --> 00:08:59,538
and I just don't... I don't know.

186
00:08:59,623 --> 00:09:00,831
How could you not know, Michael?

187
00:09:00,916 --> 00:09:06,629
I think we might have hired an outside marketing consultant.

188
00:09:08,048 --> 00:09:10,883
You might have... Okay, what firm?

189
00:09:10,967 --> 00:09:13,302
You're breaking up. Michael.

190
00:09:13,386 --> 00:09:17,806
Okay. I think it might have been an inside consultant.

191
00:09:18,600 --> 00:09:20,726
Was it the sales department?

192
00:09:21,519 --> 00:09:22,561
Yes.

193
00:09:22,646 --> 00:09:25,231
Was it Jim? No.

194
00:09:25,315 --> 00:09:27,066
Okay. Was it Dwight?

195
00:09:30,862 --> 00:09:31,946
Yes.

196
00:09:36,952 --> 00:09:40,204
Come on in. Good to see you. Have a seat.

197
00:09:42,040 --> 00:09:46,252
I just wanted to congratulate you on that great golden-ticket idea.

198
00:09:46,336 --> 00:09:47,503
That was your idea.

199
00:09:47,587 --> 00:09:49,922
Whoa, wow, who told you that? You did.

200
00:09:50,006 --> 00:09:50,965
No. Several times.

201
00:09:51,049 --> 00:09:52,174
I don't think I ever did. Over and over again.

202
00:09:52,300 --> 00:09:54,134
That was your idea, Dwight. You were dressed as Willy Wonka.

203
00:09:54,261 --> 00:09:56,345
I'm not taking credit for that. So... Wasn't my idea.

204
00:09:56,429 --> 00:09:58,180
Would love to, but I can't. Wait a second, wait a second.

205
00:09:58,265 --> 00:10:00,641
Wait a second. I wrote it down in my diary.

206
00:10:00,767 --> 00:10:03,435
You don't keep a diary. Yes, I do. You've just never seen it.

207
00:10:03,520 --> 00:10:08,524
March 4th, "Dwight came up with the best idea today about golden tickets."

208
00:10:08,608 --> 00:10:09,858
Hold on.

209
00:10:17,033 --> 00:10:20,202
March 4th,

210
00:10:20,287 --> 00:10:22,621
"Michael Scott came up with golden-ticket idea to give five customers

211
00:10:22,706 --> 00:10:24,748
"10% off for one year."

212
00:10:24,833 --> 00:10:25,916
Why do you have a diary?

213
00:10:26,001 --> 00:10:27,293
To keep secrets from my computer.

214
00:10:27,377 --> 00:10:29,378
Okay, you know what, you came up with this idea.

215
00:10:29,462 --> 00:10:33,632
I remember you told me that you loved the Willy Wonka movie

216
00:10:33,717 --> 00:10:35,301
when you were growing up. Impossible.

217
00:10:35,427 --> 00:10:38,595
Yes, you did. I wasn't given candy as a child,

218
00:10:38,680 --> 00:10:42,766
so a movie that fetishized it that much would've made no sense to me.

219
00:10:42,851 --> 00:10:47,229
Plus, we weren't allowed to see movies, so do the math.

220
00:10:50,275 --> 00:10:51,275
What are you writing?

221
00:10:51,359 --> 00:10:55,654
"Had conversation with Michael about taking credit for an idea that wasn't mine.

222
00:10:55,739 --> 00:10:57,281
"Discussed movies."

223
00:10:57,365 --> 00:11:01,535
Would you like to go to lunch with me? Just the two of us?

224
00:11:03,538 --> 00:11:05,205
With all my heart.

225
00:11:06,041 --> 00:11:08,375
You guys are throwing a lot at me.

226
00:11:08,460 --> 00:11:11,503
Well, all I'm saying is it's the first date, so just keep a respectful distance.

227
00:11:11,588 --> 00:11:12,629
Right.

228
00:11:12,714 --> 00:11:14,798
I don't think Jim means to say that you shouldn't touch her.

229
00:11:14,883 --> 00:11:15,883
No, that is what I mean.

230
00:11:15,967 --> 00:11:19,053
Kevin, a playful touch on the arm or on the back,

231
00:11:19,137 --> 00:11:22,890
it can show your interest, and it's really romantic.

232
00:11:22,974 --> 00:11:24,725
Like that? No. Stop it.

233
00:11:27,020 --> 00:11:32,232
Don't touch her, don't talk to her, don't look at her.

234
00:11:32,317 --> 00:11:34,401
He wants to be more than just friends. Put your heart out there,

235
00:11:34,486 --> 00:11:36,862
it's liable to just turn into this blackened, carbon brick,

236
00:11:36,946 --> 00:11:39,490
where it has barbeque sauce of shame and rage.

237
00:11:39,616 --> 00:11:42,409
And two hot people with a perfect relationship

238
00:11:42,494 --> 00:11:44,536
would not understand that.

239
00:11:46,247 --> 00:11:48,040
We think a lot alike.

240
00:11:48,708 --> 00:11:52,753
Sometimes you will think something, and I will say what you're thinking.

241
00:11:52,837 --> 00:11:55,047
Okay, what am I thinking right now?

242
00:11:57,384 --> 00:11:59,009
Nacho chips. No.

243
00:11:59,094 --> 00:12:01,303
I was thinking about how the skin is the largest organ of the body.

244
00:12:01,388 --> 00:12:04,306
Okay. Let me just cut to the chase, Dwight.

245
00:12:05,642 --> 00:12:07,267
I want you to fall on your sword for me.

246
00:12:07,352 --> 00:12:08,811
Oh, not gonna happen.

247
00:12:09,354 --> 00:12:10,813
I did fall on my sword once.

248
00:12:10,897 --> 00:12:14,316
I was running with it in my belt. Won't happen again.

249
00:12:14,567 --> 00:12:18,320
Why do you even want to stay at Dunder Mifflin? I mean, what's the point?

250
00:12:18,405 --> 00:12:22,574
You're cooped up in there all day. You don't get to do your farming.

251
00:12:22,659 --> 00:12:23,909
You're not dating Angela anymore.

252
00:12:23,993 --> 00:12:28,914
It doesn't matter if you get fired for taking credit for the gold-ticket idea.

253
00:12:28,998 --> 00:12:30,124
It doesn't matter.

254
00:12:30,208 --> 00:12:32,626
Me, on the other hand, I would be lost without this place.

255
00:12:32,710 --> 00:12:34,420
You, way too manly.

256
00:12:34,504 --> 00:12:35,838
What about Shoe La La?

257
00:12:35,922 --> 00:12:38,215
It's not ready yet.

258
00:12:38,299 --> 00:12:43,178
I have an idea for a fancy men's shoe store called Shoe La La.

259
00:12:44,264 --> 00:12:48,642
And it's just men's shoes for the special occasions in a man's life.

260
00:12:49,352 --> 00:12:54,273
Like the day that you get married, or the day your wife has a baby,

261
00:12:54,357 --> 00:12:56,567
or for just lounging around the house.

262
00:12:58,153 --> 00:13:00,946
May I have your attention, please? I have an announcement.

263
00:13:01,030 --> 00:13:07,786
Mr. Dwight Schrute and I just returned from a wonderful stroll together.

264
00:13:07,871 --> 00:13:12,040
And although I probably will never do it again, I had fun.

265
00:13:12,125 --> 00:13:15,836
I really had fun with my best friend, Dwight. These aren't announcements.

266
00:13:15,920 --> 00:13:19,339
Yes, they are. You just don't care about the information.

267
00:13:19,424 --> 00:13:22,092
I love working here, and I do not want to leave.

268
00:13:22,177 --> 00:13:24,052
He doesn't love it that much.

269
00:13:24,137 --> 00:13:26,096
Wouldn't be such a bad thing for him to be fired.

270
00:13:26,222 --> 00:13:30,058
Well... I mean, like we were talking about,

271
00:13:30,143 --> 00:13:32,978
that is why he has come to the conclusion...

272
00:13:33,062 --> 00:13:36,064
We discussed the fact that I'm cooped up here

273
00:13:36,149 --> 00:13:37,983
instead of experiencing the whole wide world.

274
00:13:38,109 --> 00:13:40,903
Right. You know, plowing my own acres.

275
00:13:40,987 --> 00:13:45,657
Going around shirtless all day. You know, experiencing freedom.

276
00:13:45,742 --> 00:13:49,453
That's it. That's it. You can't put a price on freedom.

277
00:13:49,579 --> 00:13:51,246
Try me. That's why you've made the decision.

278
00:13:51,414 --> 00:13:53,207
That's why you made...

279
00:13:53,333 --> 00:13:55,417
I haven't made a decision yet. You kind of have.

280
00:13:55,502 --> 00:13:58,921
No. Yes, you have.

281
00:14:03,426 --> 00:14:05,427
You cannot take the fall for him.

282
00:14:05,512 --> 00:14:06,845
He said he would do the same for me.

283
00:14:06,930 --> 00:14:11,141
He can do the same for you right now by getting fired instead of you.

284
00:14:13,520 --> 00:14:15,646
So what are you going to do?

285
00:14:16,356 --> 00:14:21,735
I'm going to go back to work, after I write you up for insubordination.

286
00:14:21,819 --> 00:14:23,111
There he is.

287
00:14:25,823 --> 00:14:28,659
Oh, my God. Dwight! Dwight, get in.

288
00:14:29,744 --> 00:14:32,829
What's it going to be? What are you going to do?

289
00:14:32,914 --> 00:14:35,874
What's it going to be? Are you going to do this thing for me?

290
00:14:36,042 --> 00:14:37,292
Dwight?

291
00:14:38,753 --> 00:14:41,004
Hey, David, how are you? Michael.

292
00:14:41,089 --> 00:14:42,798
Good to see you. Nice surprise. Hey.

293
00:14:42,882 --> 00:14:43,882
Yeah.

294
00:14:43,967 --> 00:14:47,302
Dwight and I were just having a very unfortunate conversation.

295
00:14:47,387 --> 00:14:48,470
That's too bad.

296
00:14:48,555 --> 00:14:51,056
Yes, it is. But it had to be done, didn't it?

297
00:14:51,140 --> 00:14:56,478
Hopefully, nothing that can't be undone because, Dwight, I owe you an apology.

298
00:14:56,563 --> 00:14:59,064
The head of Blue Cross just called.

299
00:14:59,148 --> 00:15:01,316
They were so excited by the golden-ticket discount

300
00:15:01,401 --> 00:15:05,404
that they have decided to make Dunder Mifflin their exclusive provider

301
00:15:05,488 --> 00:15:07,406
of all office supplies.

302
00:15:10,702 --> 00:15:12,578
Congratulations, Dwight.

303
00:15:14,998 --> 00:15:16,164
David,

304
00:15:19,419 --> 00:15:21,253
you're welcome. Yeah.

305
00:15:26,676 --> 00:15:29,595
I just want to say that this golden-ticket idea

306
00:15:29,679 --> 00:15:32,806
is one of the most brilliant signs of initiative I've ever seen at this company.

307
00:15:32,890 --> 00:15:36,643
And how about a big round of applause for Mr. Dwight Schrute?

308
00:15:39,230 --> 00:15:40,897
All right, Dwight!

309
00:15:42,150 --> 00:15:44,693
This is huge. That's what she said.

310
00:15:46,487 --> 00:15:50,032
Hey, Dwight, great idea. Dwight, Dwight, great idea.

311
00:15:51,492 --> 00:15:53,869
How'd you come up with that idea?

312
00:15:55,038 --> 00:15:56,580
Inspiration. Really?

313
00:15:56,706 --> 00:15:58,582
How were you inspired? Never know when it's going to strike.

314
00:15:58,708 --> 00:16:01,001
How did it pop into your head, Dwight? Just boom.

315
00:16:01,085 --> 00:16:02,628
Just give me the details of how that happened.

316
00:16:02,712 --> 00:16:05,547
You're talking to Dwight Schrute, the biggest Wonka fan I know.

317
00:16:05,632 --> 00:16:07,633
I mean, you've been talking about that movie for years.

318
00:16:07,717 --> 00:16:08,884
What?

319
00:16:08,968 --> 00:16:10,469
You know what, I even made fun of you

320
00:16:10,553 --> 00:16:13,055
when you dressed up as Willy Wonka to pitch this idea,

321
00:16:13,139 --> 00:16:14,473
and, for that, I apologize.

322
00:16:14,557 --> 00:16:16,475
Apology rejected.

323
00:16:16,559 --> 00:16:18,810
Thanks so much for helping the company, Dwight.

324
00:16:18,895 --> 00:16:20,187
Oh, Pam.

325
00:16:20,271 --> 00:16:21,313
Good work, kid.

326
00:16:21,397 --> 00:16:22,648
Thanks, old man.

327
00:16:22,732 --> 00:16:23,774
This is... This is great.

328
00:16:23,858 --> 00:16:26,985
Okay, look, I want to get you on the horn with the marketing people in New York.

329
00:16:27,070 --> 00:16:28,528
- They should meet you. - Great idea. Yeah.

330
00:16:28,613 --> 00:16:31,573
Pam, could you set up a call in there in about 15 minutes, please?

331
00:16:31,658 --> 00:16:33,450
Sure. David. David.

332
00:16:33,576 --> 00:16:35,077
David. David. 'Cause I've got this idea for a...

333
00:16:35,203 --> 00:16:37,037
Yeah? Could I be part of the meeting also?

334
00:16:37,121 --> 00:16:38,330
You probably have too much to do around here.

335
00:16:38,414 --> 00:16:40,874
I don't want to take up your time with another meeting.

336
00:16:40,958 --> 00:16:43,460
Yeah, let's face it, this meeting sounds like it's Wonka fans only.

337
00:16:43,586 --> 00:16:44,670
He's never seen the movie.

338
00:16:44,754 --> 00:16:46,505
All right.

339
00:16:53,971 --> 00:16:57,349
Hey, guys, I have with me Dwight Schrute. Dwight, take it away.

340
00:16:57,433 --> 00:17:01,812
Okay, guys, listen up. Here's the deal. I love candy, sweet, sugary candy,

341
00:17:01,896 --> 00:17:03,271
from the second it touches my tongue

342
00:17:03,356 --> 00:17:05,524
to the moment it's metabolized by my stomach acid,

343
00:17:05,608 --> 00:17:08,694
so naturally I liked Willy Wonka.

344
00:17:08,778 --> 00:17:10,862
Wait, do you guys hear that?

345
00:17:10,947 --> 00:17:14,199
There's not a dog listening in, is there? I hear panting.

346
00:17:14,283 --> 00:17:16,159
No. No dogs.

347
00:17:16,244 --> 00:17:19,454
So I love Willy Wonka.

348
00:17:19,539 --> 00:17:23,458
The golden-ticket scene is so inspiring to me

349
00:17:23,543 --> 00:17:25,502
that that's where I came up with that idea.

350
00:17:25,586 --> 00:17:27,713
There is no movie called Willy Wonka!

351
00:17:27,797 --> 00:17:29,548
It's called Willy Wonka & the Chocolate Factory.

352
00:17:29,632 --> 00:17:32,050
It's actually based on a book called Charlie and the Chocolate Factory.

353
00:17:32,135 --> 00:17:34,386
I can't vouch for that. Pam.

354
00:17:34,470 --> 00:17:37,597
But I do know this. He is a liar. He has taken my idea.

355
00:17:37,682 --> 00:17:39,891
That is my idea. We are going to call you back, guys.

356
00:17:39,976 --> 00:17:43,270
What is going on here? Okay, here's what happened, David.

357
00:17:43,354 --> 00:17:44,771
It was all my idea.

358
00:17:44,856 --> 00:17:47,858
You called me, and you were really angry at me,

359
00:17:47,942 --> 00:17:50,277
and I got scared, so I had Dwight take the fall.

360
00:17:50,361 --> 00:17:52,738
But now it turns out that it's a great idea,

361
00:17:52,822 --> 00:17:55,407
and Dwight will not confess. Can you believe that?

362
00:17:55,491 --> 00:17:56,742
No. No. It is my idea.

363
00:17:56,826 --> 00:17:59,244
How dare you, Dwight? It is my idea.

364
00:17:59,328 --> 00:18:00,704
I'm filled with good ideas. Are you?

365
00:18:00,788 --> 00:18:02,247
Thousands of good ideas. You are?

366
00:18:02,331 --> 00:18:03,582
Yeah. Good ideas?

367
00:18:03,666 --> 00:18:05,375
Hey, did you come up with Toilet Buddy?

368
00:18:05,460 --> 00:18:08,170
It's a net, a circular net you put inside the toilet

369
00:18:08,254 --> 00:18:12,215
to catch all of your change and your wallet from falling into the toilet,

370
00:18:12,300 --> 00:18:14,384
formerly known as Toilet Guard. Horse Boat.

371
00:18:14,510 --> 00:18:17,220
Oh, please. A canoe built around your horse

372
00:18:17,305 --> 00:18:20,390
so you can go from riding to water travel without slowing down.

373
00:18:20,516 --> 00:18:21,975
Horse Boat. Toilet Sponge.

374
00:18:22,059 --> 00:18:23,935
It's a hollowed out sponge... Give me a break.

375
00:18:24,020 --> 00:18:27,898
That is more absorbent and softer than toilet paper.

376
00:18:27,982 --> 00:18:30,275
I have a lot of toilet ideas. That's 'cause they're easy.

377
00:18:30,359 --> 00:18:32,068
They're not easy. Guys. Guys.

378
00:18:32,153 --> 00:18:33,904
Everybody... Women's urinal.

379
00:18:34,030 --> 00:18:39,409
Everybody has to go to the bathroom! Enough. Please, enough.

380
00:18:39,494 --> 00:18:43,955
Well, is this true, Dwight? I mean, is this true?

381
00:18:44,040 --> 00:18:49,628
Yes, it's Michael's idea that he forced on me on threat of death.

382
00:18:49,712 --> 00:18:51,046
Thank you.

383
00:18:51,964 --> 00:18:53,715
I don't know what... What do you want me to do now, Michael?

384
00:18:53,800 --> 00:18:56,134
What am I supposed to do now?

385
00:18:56,219 --> 00:18:57,969
Well, David, I will be honest with you.

386
00:18:58,054 --> 00:19:01,056
I do want the credit without any of the blame.

387
00:19:02,225 --> 00:19:05,685
Okay. I am going back to New York.

388
00:19:07,897 --> 00:19:11,066
Pam, do me a favor, don't send me those notes.

389
00:19:11,150 --> 00:19:12,234
Okay.

390
00:19:13,736 --> 00:19:15,070
I am gone.

391
00:19:20,243 --> 00:19:22,828
When they look back on this day in the history books,

392
00:19:22,912 --> 00:19:26,373
all that will be remembered is that I had a good idea.

393
00:19:26,457 --> 00:19:29,251
And that's what I'm going to write down in my diary.

394
00:19:29,335 --> 00:19:31,169
And that is what I want you to write down in yours.

395
00:19:31,254 --> 00:19:34,297
Oh, I am. In my own words.

396
00:19:35,341 --> 00:19:36,383
I want to see it. No.

397
00:19:36,467 --> 00:19:37,509
I want to see that. No, Michael.

398
00:19:37,593 --> 00:19:39,678
Give me the diary. No. No!

399
00:19:45,393 --> 00:19:47,352
Hi. Lynn,

400
00:19:47,436 --> 00:19:50,647
I'm just going to say to you everything that I'm thinking.

401
00:19:50,731 --> 00:19:54,276
Okay. I think you have the best smile.

402
00:19:55,820 --> 00:19:58,905
I'd like to take you out to dinner and a movie.

403
00:19:59,949 --> 00:20:00,949
Okay.

404
00:20:01,784 --> 00:20:02,826
Nice...

405
00:20:04,579 --> 00:20:05,745
boobs.

406
00:20:14,380 --> 00:20:16,673
Well, it's his funeral, so...

407
00:20:20,761 --> 00:20:21,761
Ding dong.

408
00:20:21,846 --> 00:20:22,971
Who is it?

409
00:20:23,055 --> 00:20:24,890
KGB. All right.

410
00:20:24,974 --> 00:20:27,142
I just got out of the shower. I'll be one second.

411
00:20:27,226 --> 00:20:29,644
When you are done, open the door.

412
00:20:33,441 --> 00:20:34,900
Hello in there?

413
00:20:34,984 --> 00:20:36,735
Yeah, I'm late for work so I have to brush my teeth.

414
00:20:36,819 --> 00:20:38,028
It's a whole routine.

415
00:20:38,112 --> 00:20:40,155
We have other houses to visit.

416
00:20:40,239 --> 00:20:43,575
If you want to come back, then that would be fine.

417
00:20:43,659 --> 00:20:45,660
We'll come back at...

418
00:20:45,995 --> 00:20:47,704
How's 4:45?

419
00:20:47,788 --> 00:20:49,623
I get home from work around 6:00.

420
00:20:51,751 --> 00:20:53,668
15?

421
00:20:54,629 --> 00:20:57,088
You can try. That might work.

422
00:20:57,173 --> 00:20:59,299
Very well, we will 15.

423
00:20:59,383 --> 00:21:00,717
All right.

